# Introducing HTML + AI

####  Now that you have been introduced to user experience, let’s dive into how the web apps are made.

## HTML

#### **HTML (HyperText Markup Language)** is the code that gives a web page its structure. It tells the browser what to show — like headings, paragraphs, images, and links — so users can see and interact with the content.

<img src="https://images.code.org/1a60c7acc114352abd9a7e83320ddaa9-image-1763396009343.png" alt="html code with head, body , header, main and footer labeled" width="750px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px;">

#### *Notice how each section has an opening tag and a closing tag with a back slash.This tells the computer where each section begins and ends, so it knows how to organize and display the content correctly.*

<div style="clear:both;"></div>
<div
  style="
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin: 16px 0;
    background: #b2dee3;
  "
>
  <img 
    src="https://images.code.org/59c9b8a356ff4e56ec8c802ba5718b37-image-1684269154206.png" 
    alt="A clipboard labeled TO DO" 
    width="40px"
    style="margin-right: 12px;"
  >

  <p style="margin: 12px; font-size: 16px;">
    <strong>Look at the tags. Can you guess what any of them do?</strong>
  </p>
</div>


## The Structure of an HTML Page

- #### **The `<head>` Section** -The head holds information about the page, not what’s shown on the page itself.

- #### **The `<body>` Section** is what users see on the webpage.
	- #### The `<header>` is the top portion of a website.
    - #### The `<main>` section holds the core content of the site. In this example, there is a `<section>` with a subheading (`<h2>`), a paragraph (`<p>`), and a list (`<ul>`with `<li>` items).
    - #### The `<footer>` section closes out the web page. This example has a copyright line. 
    
## Next Up

#### Let's take a look at some common HTML tags. 
